==English==

===Etymology===
From the participle stem of {{der|en|la-lat|*aequilībrō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/iːkwɪˈlɪbɹeɪt/|/iːˈkwɪlɪbɹeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-equilibrate.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[balance]], or bring into [[equilibrium]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Giancarlo Gandolfo|title=Economic Dynamics|edition=4th|publisher=Springer|page=303
|passage=[Walras] was well aware of the indirect influences of the changes in the other prices, but he assumed that these influences were some  '''equilibrating''' and some disequilibrating, so that up to a certain point they cancelled each other out; hence the prevailing effect war the stabilizing one of the ‘own’ price.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[balance]], to be in a state of equilibrium.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|equilibrize}}

